# Reviewing a change in this repository

This repository has a review tool configured. Run it before you push, so that what
a human sees on the pull request is not the first time anyone looked at the change.

## When to run it

- **Before pushing a branch.** Findings you fix now cost nobody's attention.
- **Before opening a pull request**, if the branch was already pushed.
- **After changing anything in response to review comments**, because a fix
  frequently introduces the next problem.

Not on every file save. It spawns compilers and scanners, and a few seconds of
somebody else's patience is the thing it is protecting.

## How to run it

```
bash .agent/skills/code-review/scripts/review.sh --staged
```

That reviews what is staged. To review a whole branch against its base:

```
bash .agent/skills/code-review/scripts/review.sh --base {{ .DefaultBranch }}
```

## Reading the output

The output separates two kinds of thing, and the difference decides what you do
about each:

**Facts.** Type errors, lint violations, layering violations, failing tests,
unused exports, vulnerable dependencies, this repository's own convention rules.
These came from a compiler or a pattern match. If one appears, the code has the
problem — fix it, or say in the pull request why the rule does not apply here.

**Suggestions.** Anything marked as medium or low confidence, and anything in the
`arch/` or `quality/` categories. These came from a model reading your diff. They
are frequently right and they are judgements. Read each one, act on the ones you
agree with, and ignore the rest without ceremony. A suggestion you disagree with
is not a finding you have to argue against.

Confidence is the marker: high-confidence findings are facts about the code,
everything else is an opinion about it.

## What not to do

- **Do not fix a finding by suppressing it.** Adding an ignore comment, widening a
  type to `any`, or deleting a test to make the runner pass converts a visible
  problem into an invisible one.
- **Do not act on a finding about code this change did not touch.** The tool scopes
  itself to the diff; if something slips through, it is not this change's to fix.
- **Do not treat an empty result as permission.** It means nothing was found, which
  is a much weaker statement than "this is good".
